{"t":1794332960,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"bec43be0-701b-40bd-8d78-48a1bed8750c","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-10T14:15:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 101415\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n915 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes made this morning to the forecast. After\ncoordination, decision made to not issue a fire weather SPS for\ntoday. See fire weather section below for more details.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow Friday\n     into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow \nFriday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\nShortwave ridging coupled with westerly downslope flow will\nallow for mild afternoons today and Wednesday. High temperatures\ntoday will be around 70. Wednesday will be a few degs cooler \ndue to more clouds and lighter SE winds. Very dry today with \nmarginal fire weather concerns. See Fire Weather section below. \n\nRain is expected to move in by early Thursday as low pressure\ncrosses the Appalachians and over to the Mid-Atlantic coast.\nThere weren't any significant changes seen at the sfc or at  \n500 mb compared to previous runs with the system expected to \nimpact the area Thursday into Friday.\n\nBehind this system, much colder air will follow on brisk NNW\nwinds with the Allegheny Mountains seeing their first \naccumulating snow of the season starting late Thu night and \ncontinuing through Fri evening. A few inches of accumulation are\nlikely falling roughly in a 24-hr period from 06Z Fri through\n06Z Sat. \n\nRemaining colder than normal Fri through the first half of next\nweek under brisk NNW flow between low pressure well offshore and\nstrong Canadian high pressure to our west.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /14Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nLighter winds today than yesterday, but potentially still \ngusting 15-20 kt range during the afternoon. Winds shift to the\nsoutheast Wed with even lighter speeds.\n\nRain is expected to arrive by daybreak Thu and continue through\nthe day before it starts exiting to the northeast Thu night, but\nmay linger into Fri morning across northeast Maryland. \n\nBrisk NNW winds gusting 20-30 kt will be the biggest impact\nFri-Sun. MVFR/IFR conditions are likely Thursday into Fri\nmorning with a broken stratocu layer persisting all weekend into\nearly next week.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nWinds remain generally below SCA through Thursday. Winds\nstrengthen Thu night through the weekend and peak in strength\nSaturday with SCA conditions expected Thu night through at least\nthe first part of next week. A period of gale force winds is\npossible Saturday due to tight pressure gradient between deep\nlow offshore and strong Canadian high pressure over the MS River\nValley.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nFire weather conditions could be locally sensitive today given\nthe well above normal temperatures and minimum RH values in the\n25 to 35 percent range. Winds are going to be lighter than\nyesterday, with gusts around 15-20 mph at times. The Potomac\nHighlands and northern Shenandoah Valley are the main areas to\nclosely monitor, but overall conditions are marginal so have \nopted not to issue a fire weather SPS today.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/KRR\nAVIATION...LFR/KRR\nMARINE...LFR/KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794332890,"days":{"2026-11-10":{"hi":69,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-11":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":63,"lo":42,"pop":66,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":50,"lo":36,"pop":32,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-14":{"hi":50,"lo":36,"po